DATA = "# Frozen implementation prompt — SPSCGG_12344_R1\n\n## 1. Mission and non-negotiable behaviour\nImplement persistent Windows BYOK API-key storage in the existing daemon credential service. Save must complete only after durable, OS-user-bound protection; reopening Settings, restarting, and logging back into the same Windows account must retain the selected metadata and secret. List/get may expose only configured status and last four characters. Missing/inaccessible credentials fail explicitly; a BYOK run must never silently transition to cloud billing. Preserve existing macOS Keychain, Linux Secret Service, credential update/delete rollback, local authorization middleware, cloud/local-CLI defaults, and no unintended billing. Do not store plaintext secrets in app JSON/SQLite/logs, process argv, URL query strings, or readable files.\n\n## 2. Source and interface map\n`apps/daemon/src/byok/credential-service.ts` exports `ByokSecretBackend` (`available/set/get/delete`), `ByokCredentialService`, `createPlatformByokSecretBackend`, `assertProfileId` and serial metadata writes. `apps/daemon/src/routes/byok-credentials.ts` uses `requireLocalDaemonRequest` on all credential routes. `apps/daemon/src/routes/runs.ts` checks BYOK profile admission; `apps/daemon/src/runtimes/byok-opencode.ts` builds the dedicated BYOK provider; `apps/daemon/src/runtimes/launch.ts` launches runtime. `apps/daemon/src/app-config.ts`, web `SettingsDialog.tsx`, BYOK fields/preflight, `apps/web/src/providers/daemon.ts`, contracts `packages/contracts/src/api/{app-config,byok-credentials,connectionTest}.ts`, and existing daemon/web tests define the UI and typed API. Only a 38-file excerpt is supplied; full Electron bootstrap and native Windows installation are unavailable.\n\n## 3. Trust-boundary and data-flow map\nUI-entered key flows through authenticated local credential route to `upsert`, then via anonymous stdin of a non-shell Windows PowerShell process to DPAPI CurrentUser. Only DPAPI ciphertext is stored in `dataDir/byok/secrets/<validated-id>.bin`; profile metadata stays in `profiles.json`, with no secret. The Windows account is the security principal; DPAPI decryption is scoped to that account. `profileId` comes from API/user metadata: enforce the existing restrictive `PROFILE_ID_PATTERN` before filesystem paths; filenames must remain beneath the configured secrets folder. PowerShell command is constant, not derived from input, and its input/output are bounded. On read, encrypted blob flows into DPAPI and plaintext back through private process stdout into the runtime's existing in-memory BYOK provider, never to logs. Existing user-controlled provider URL remains constrained by `normalizeProfileInput`; no new network requests. Access control is the existing `requireLocalDaemonRequest`; do not replace it. Unverified: Windows ACL inheritance and installed PowerShell availability, runtime restart and real user logon.\n\n## 4. Security invariants\n* No successful `set` until DPAPI encryption succeeds and ciphertext is atomically committed (unique temporary file, rename; cleanup on errors). No plaintext secret written to disk, passed on argv, or put in errors/logs.\n* `get` must reject corrupt/oversized ciphertext and DPAPI failures instead of behaving as a missing key; missing file alone returns null. Encrypt/decrypt bind DPAPI entropy to service and profile ID, preventing profile substitution. Reject invalid profile IDs at the backend boundary, not only service calls.\n* `available` checks native Windows platform and executable probe, not fake availability on Linux test hosts. Backend exceptions are generic and never echo command stderr, output, or secrets. Bound subprocess lifetime and output; kill on timeout/overflow, handle `error` and `close` only once.\n* Deletion removes the encrypted blob; metadata and rollback stay governed by `ByokCredentialService` mutation queue. Never silently fall back to an unencrypted backend or cloud runtime. Preserve legacy Windows cleanup path semantics by using the same `.bin` folder.\n* Existing Linux/macOS paths and cloud/local CLI routing remain unchanged.\n\n## 5. Architecture choices and forbidden patterns\nOption A (selected): use built-in Windows `powershell.exe` to call .NET `System.Security.Cryptography.ProtectedData` with `DataProtectionScope.CurrentUser`; pass base64 JSON via stdin with constant script text; persist only ciphertext in app data via atomic Node filesystem writes. This avoids an external native dependency and preserves retired blob location, at the cost of a bounded child process and inability to perform native Windows tests here. Option B: Windows Credential Manager via a native addon or P/Invoke wrapper; OS-native named records, but missing native bindings or embedded C# compilation/architecture dependencies add deployment and command complexity. Do not use `ConvertFrom-SecureString` with a hardcoded shared key, insecure storage, shell interpolation, secrets in `-Command`/`-EncodedCommand` arguments, `exec`, `cmd.exe`, unbounded subprocess buffering, error details from PowerShell, or optimistic success status.\n\n## 6. Executable acceptance and adversarial tests\nTest profile save/list/resolve acro"
